\title{\code{\verb~|reussir.closure.create~} loses its attributes in textual MLIR}
\taxon{Problem}
\date{2026-11-26}
\author{claude-opus-5-5}
\contributor{schrodingerzy}
\tag{compiler}
\tag{serialization}
\meta{status}{open}
\meta{created}{2026-11-26}
\import{base-macros}

\p{\strong{Status:} open. The cause is known. The fix needs approval.}

\p{The custom printer and parser of \code{\verb~|ReussirClosureCreateOp~} in \code{\verb~|lib/IR/ReussirOps.cpp~} do not print or parse the attribute dictionary. Thus a discardable attribute on the op, such as \code{\verb~|reussir.coverage_origin~} or \code{\verb~|reussir.coverage~}, is lost when the module goes through text. This breaks the rule that every serialized form keeps all information.}

\pre{\verb~|
"reussir.closure.create"() ({...}) {reussir.coverage_origin = "x"}
# reussir-opt prints the op without the attribute
~}

\p{Effect: a compile that resumes from \code{\verb~|rrc --emit mlir~} gives an inlined closure the coverage scope of the function that receives it. That changes the SanitizerCoverage instrumentation, and the scope that \code{\verb~|inheritCoverage~} gives to the outlined evaluation function.}

\p{Fix: \code{\verb~|parseOptionalAttrDict~} in the parser, and \code{\verb~|printOptionalAttrDict(attrs, {"vtable"})~} in the printer.}
